## Streaming Large Uploads in ASP.NET Core 8 Minimal API

`IFormFile` buffers the entire body into memory — that's why it OOMs. Instead, read the raw multipart stream section-by-section using `Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities.MultipartReader`.

### Key changes needed

**1. Disable the default form body size limit and request size limit:**

```csharp
// Program.cs — service & server config
builder.WebHost.ConfigureKestrel(o =>
{
    o.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024; // 2 GB
});

builder.Services.Configure<FormOptions>(o =>
{
    o.MultipartBodyLengthLimit = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024;
});
```

**2. Stream directly to disk with `MultipartReader`:**

```csharp
app.MapPost("/upload", async (HttpRequest request) =>
{
    if (!request.HasFormContentType ||
        !MediaTypeHeaderValue.TryParse(request.ContentType, out var mt) ||
        string.IsNullOrEmpty(mt.Boundary.Value))
    {
        return Results.BadRequest("Expected a multipart/form-data request.");
    }

    var boundary = HeaderUtilities.RemoveQuotes(mt.Boundary.Value).ToString();
    var reader   = new MultipartReader(boundary, request.Body);

    MultipartSection? section;
    while ((section = await reader.ReadNextSectionAsync()) is not null)
    {
        if (!ContentDispositionHeaderValue.TryParse(
                section.ContentDisposition, out var cd) ||
            !cd.IsFileDisposition())
        {
            continue; // skip non-file fields
        }

        var trustedName = Path.GetRandomFileName();          // never trust the client name
        var savePath    = Path.Combine("/tmp/uploads", trustedName);

        await using var fs = new FileStream(
            savePath,
            FileMode.Create,
            FileAccess.Write,
            FileShare.None,
            bufferSize: 81920,           // 80 KB I/O buffer
            useAsync: true);

        await section.Body.CopyToAsync(fs);
    }

    return Results.Ok("Upload complete.");
})
.DisableAntiforgery();        // minimal APIs require this for multipart
```

### Why this works

| | `IFormFile` | `MultipartReader` |
|---|---|---|
| **Memory** | Buffers entire file | Streams in ~80 KB chunks |
| **2 GB file** | OOM crash | ~80 KB constant RAM |
| **Mechanism** | Model binding reads body first | You read the raw stream directly |

### Don't forget

- **`[DisableRequestSizeLimit]`** — not available on minimal APIs; use the Kestrel setting above.
- **`DisableAntiforgery()`** — required or the framework rejects multipart POSTs.
- **Sanitize filenames** — `Path.GetRandomFileName()` avoids path-traversal attacks.
- **Add a size guard** if you want to abort early per-section:

```csharp
// wrap section.Body with a size-limiting stream
var limited = new SizeLimitedStream(section.Body, maxBytes: 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024);
await limited.CopyToAsync(fs);
```

This pattern keeps memory flat regardless of upload size.
